{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1429 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2cd7ff6bb2a598d4cf1477b443aef765902ca48ebf5b15cff396923f25b22a6c" :edb/db-before-t 1428 :edb/db-after-t 1429 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534741 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:35:48.647Z" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192254 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534741 false] [17592186192254 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192254 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192254 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{∞}\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 2^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2})]_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2}) - \\arctan (\\sqrt{3})) \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2}) - \\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2})] - π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/cb07856ca51f2e4c4c57cae7ba76729b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{∞}\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= 3lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2})] - π \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2} - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 false] [17592186192255 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192255 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534741 false] [17592186192255 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1/4}^{∞}\\frac{2}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx &= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1/4}^{a}(1)/(1 + (4x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (4x)]_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (4a) - \\arctan (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] - (-\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] + \\frac{π}{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan{(4x)}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/6f825ead10f726d4996ce7df4d607c5f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1/4}^{∞}\\frac{2}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] + \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 false] [17592186192255 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192282 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534741 false] [17592186192282 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192282 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}\\frac{4}{1 + x^{2}}dx &= 4 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)∫_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (0) - \\arctan (a)] \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (0 - lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (a)]) \\\\\n&=-4lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (a)]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny = \\arctan{x}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/6c01ba2881dcb5e3c67965f2a0924e63.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}\\frac{4}{1 + x^{2}}dx &= -4lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (a)] \\\\\n&=-4(-\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 false] [17592186192282 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192283 1100 "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192283 1100 "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534741 false] [17592186192283 1145 "First, we substitute $u = x^2$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2x}{1 + x^{4}}dx &= ∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2x}{1 + x^{4}}dx &= ∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (a) - \\arctan (1)] \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (a)] - \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan(x)\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/e22f4e5f43eb489fe5da8cd1e73d5eab.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2x}{1 + x^{4}}dx &= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (a)] - \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 false] [17592186192283 1145 "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306911 13194139534741 true] [17592186192659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306918 13194139534741 true] [17592186192659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306925 13194139534741 true] [17592186192659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306977 13194139534741 true] [17592186192659 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306980 13194139534741 true] [17592186192660 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306932 13194139534741 true] [17592186192660 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306939 13194139534741 true] [17592186192660 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306967 13194139534741 true] [17592186192660 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306970 13194139534741 true] [17592186192660 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306983 13194139534741 true] [17592186192661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306904 13194139534741 true] [17592186192661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306946 13194139534741 true] [17592186192661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306953 13194139534741 true] [17592186192661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306986 13194139534741 true] [17592186192662 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306960 13194139534741 true] [17592186192662 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306974 13194139534741 true] [17592186192662 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306989 13194139534741 true] [17592186192947 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534741 true] [17592186192947 1100 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186192947 1100 "What is $∫_{- ∞}^{1}\\frac{6t}{t^{4} + 3}dt$?" 13194139534741 false] [17592186192947 1145 "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186193065 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534741 true] [17592186193065 1100 "Use the substitution $u = e^{x}$ to evaluate $∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{x}}{1 + 3e^{2x}}dx$." 13194139534741 false] [17592186193065 1100 "Use the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534741 true] [17592186193065 1145 "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 